          "blocks": {
            "intro_text": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Most of us buy our turkeys frozen, and common practice says to thaw the bird fully before roasting. That takes days in the fridge, even up to a week, or 12 hours in tepid, often unsafe water. And that's before the turkey spends several hours in the oven.</p><p>Chris Young skips the thaw. The frozen turkey goes into a low oven, gets salted once the skin is wet to the touch, and roasts until the breast is done. It keeps more of its natural juices than a slowly thawed bird. <strong>Already thawed yours? The recipe below has a version for that too.</strong></p>",
