                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_1_h": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<h3>Frozen is juicier</h3>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "normal",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "h5",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_1_p": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>During slow, traditional thawing, ice crystals grow between the muscle fibers and squeeze juice out of the meat. When that ice melts, it flows out as moisture loss. The slower the thaw, the more juice seeps out. Thawing fast, in the oven, means less moisture loss and juicier meat.</p>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "none",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_2_h": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<h3>Low and slow, like sous vide</h3>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "normal",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "h5",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_2_p": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>A low oven gives you the best parts of sous vide cooking: really juicy, evenly cooked meat. Cooking in an oven instead of a water bath also dries out the skin, which makes it easier to brown and crisp later.</p>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "none",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_3_h": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<h3>Rest first, then crisp</h3>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "normal",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "h5",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_3_p": {
              "type": "text",
              "settings": {
                "text": "<p>Think of the skin like chicharrones. The slow roast breaks its collagen down into gelatin. A rest of at least 30 minutes lets the steam in the skin condense. Then a 500°F oven turns that water back into steam, stretching the skin thin, taut and ready to crisp in 5 to 10 minutes.</p>",
                "width": "100%",
                "max_width": "none",
                "alignment": "left",
                "type_preset": "rte",
                "font": "var(--font-body--family)",
                "font_size": "1rem",
                "line_height": "normal",
                "letter_spacing": "normal",
                "case": "none",
                "wrap": "pretty",
                "text_color": "{{ settings.color_palette.color7 }}",
                "background": false,
                "background_color": "#00000026",
                "corner_radius": 0,
                "padding-block-start": 0,
                "padding-block-end": 0,
                "padding-inline-start": 0,
                "padding-inline-end": 0
              },
              "blocks": {}
            },
            "why_3_img": {
              "type": "image",
              "settings": {
                "image": "shopify://shop_images/02_why_it_works_recipe_card_-_turkey-carving.jpg",
                "link": "",
